            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.138,
            0.9,
            0.174
          ],
          "content": "**9.** If Q(0, 1) is equidistant from $\\mathrm{P}(5, -3)$ and $\\mathrm { R } ( x ,   6 )$ , find the values of x. Also find the distances QR and PR."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.179,
            0.901,
            0.216
          ],
          "content": "**10.** Find a relation between x and y such that the point $( x , y )$ is equidistant from the point (3, 6) and (– 3, 4)."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.235,
            0.324,
            0.255
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7.3 Section Formula"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.263,
            0.517,
            0.493
          ],
          "content": "Let us recall the situation in Section 7.2. Suppose a telephone company wants to position a relay tower at P between A and B is such a way that the distance of the tower from B is twice its distance from A. If P lies on AB, it will divide AB in the ratio 1 : 2 (see Fig. 7.9). If we take A as the origin O, and 1 km as one unit on both the axis, the coordinates of B will be (36, 15). In order to know the position of the tower, we must know the coordinates of P. How do we find these coordinates?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.542,
            0.287,
            0.898,
            0.45
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.5,
            0.902,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": "Let the coordinates of P be $( x ,   y )$ Draw perpendiculars from P and B to the x-axis, meeting it in D and E, respectively. Draw PC perpendicular to BE. Then, by the AA similarity criterion, studied in Chapter 6,   POD and $\\Delta   \\mathrm { B P C }$ are similar."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.564,
            0.579,
            0.608
          ],
          "content": "Therefore , $\\frac{OD}{PC} = \\frac{OP}{PB} = \\frac{1}{2}$ , and $\\frac{\\mathrm{PD}}{\\mathrm{BC}} = \\frac{\\mathrm{OP}}{\\mathrm{PB}} = \\frac{1}{2}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.613,
            0.451,
            0.653
          ],
          "content": "So, $\\frac { x } { 3 6   -   x }   =   \\frac { 1 } { 2 }$ and $\\frac{y}{15 - y} \\div \\frac{1}{2}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.656,
            0.482,
            0.675
          ],
          "content": "These equations give $x = 1 2$ and $y = 5$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.681,
            0.516,
            0.719
          ],
          "content": "You can check that P(12, 5) meets the condition that $\\mathrm{OP}:\\mathrm{PB}=1:2$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.726,
            0.516,
            0.784
          ],
          "content": "Now let us use the understanding that you may have developed through this example to obtain the general formula."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.788,
            0.517,
            0.898
          ],
          "content": "Consider any two points $\\mathrm { A } ( x _ { 1 } , y _ { 1 } )$ and $\\mathbf { B } ( x _ { _ 2 } ,   y _ { _ 2 } )$ and assume that $\\mathrm { ~ P ~ } ( x ,   y )$ divides AB internally in the ratio $m _ { _ 1 } : m _ { _ 2 } ,$ i.e., $\\frac { \\mathrm { P A } } { \\mathrm { P B } }   =   \\frac { m _ { 1 } } { m _ { 2 } }$ (see Fig. 7.10)."
        },
        {
